---
import { orgPaths } from '../../../lib/tenant-paths';
export const getStaticPaths = orgPaths;

import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../../../components/PageHead.astro';
import { activeOrg } from '../../../lib/tenant';

const org = activeOrg(Astro);
---

<Base
  title="Ethics and eligibility"
  description="Human participants, animals, hazardous materials, AI disclosure, and work presented elsewhere first."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Publication"
    title="Ethics and eligibility"
    lede="Most of this is the same set of rules the competition already imposes. The part people get wrong is the timing, not the paperwork."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="note stop">
      <h4>Dates first</h4>
      <p>
        Required approvals have to be signed and dated before experimentation begins. A
        form signed afterward cannot be repaired later. If you have already started, say so
        now rather than at submission.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h2>Human participants</h2>
      <p>
        Surveys count. Interviews count. Anything where a person supplies data about
        themselves counts, including your classmates and including anonymous responses. The
        approvals have to be in place before you collect anything, and no identifiable image
        or record of a participant appears in what we publish.
      </p>

      <h2>Vertebrate animals</h2>
      <p>
        Additional requirements apply and they are strict. Invertebrates sit outside the
        vertebrate rules but still require you to describe your handling honestly.
      </p>

      <h2>Hazardous materials, agents, and devices</h2>
      <p>
        Biological agents, controlled substances, and hazardous chemicals or equipment all
        pull in extra review. Describe what you used and under whose supervision.
      </p>

      <h2>Work done in an outside lab</h2>
      <p>
        Working in a university or company lab is allowed and is not something to hide. It
        does trigger additional requirements, and it makes the contributions statement more
        important rather than less: name what was yours and what was the lab's.
      </p>

      <h2>Disclosing AI use</h2>
      <p>
        Say where you used it and for what. Using a model to draft code, tidy prose, or
        explore ideas is normal and disclosable. Presenting generated text or generated
        results as your own work is not, and it is the kind of thing a reviewer asks about
        directly.
      </p>

      <h2>Plagiarism</h2>
      <p>
        Words and figures that are not yours are cited and attributed, every time. A figure
        redrawn from a paper still credits the paper.
      </p>

      <h2>Work presented or published elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        Tell us before you submit. Some conferences hold rights that make republishing the
        full text a real problem rather than a hypothetical one. Where that is the case we
        publish a record that points at the authoritative version instead, which is better
        for you than a rights dispute later.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking</h2>
      <p>
        If you are not sure whether something applies to you, write to {org.name} and ask
        before you start. That question has never once been a nuisance, and asking it late
        is how projects get disqualified.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
